_:n7cddb9cb9a3747e5be1b8f1838d3299fxb0	http://schema.org/description	"<p>Founded in 1996 thanks to the joint effort of the Archaeological Superintendence for the Marche region and the Municipal Administration, the Archaeological Museum of Arcevia is located in the restored and modernized premises adjacent to the Cloister of San Francesco.</p> <p>The museum serves as a district museum, bringing together materials from the municipal territory related to various historical periods and cultural aspects, from prehistory to the threshold of the Roman era. It houses a selection of materials from the Paleolithic sites of Ponte di Pietra and Nidastore, from the fortified Eneolithic village of Conelle, from the stratified settlement of Cava Giacometti spanning from the late Neolithic to the Bronze Age, and from the hill settlement from the end of the Bronze Age at Monte Croce Guardia.</p><p>Also noteworthy are the Etruscan imported ceramics and bronzes from the Gallic necropolis of Montefortino, precious goldsmith works, votive statuettes, and burial sets. Finally, 3 stone steles are exhibited, two of which were found in the Arcevia area and the third near Fabriano; they bear the sculpted representation of the porta ditis (gate of the underworld) with a small niche for cultic-sacred purposes on the back. The museum is equipped with an explanatory and educational apparatus that also makes use of audiovisual and multimedia resources.</p>"
_:n7cddb9cb9a3747e5be1b8f1838d3299fxb0	http://schema.org/keywords	"Archaeology"
_:n7cddb9cb9a3747e5be1b8f1838d3299fxb0	http://schema.org/name	"Archaeological Museum of Arcevia"
_:na1f21f1e030f4c34826db8a014c682b5xb0	http://schema.org/description	"<p>The Museum preserves, protects, studies and enhances one of the highest expressions of Piero della Francesca&#39;s work. Piero della Francesca&#39;s art and technique are also explained through digital media and videos, and the visitor arrives prepared, at the end of the tour, to view the work, kept in a protective case.</p><p> In the Museum there is also the &quot;Madonna del latte&quot;, a smaller fourteenth-century fresco by an unknown local author, depicting a Madonna with Child, found in 1911 by the restorer Domenico Fiscali on the occasion of the removal of the Madonna del Parto. The museum has an internal shop and a garden, a real balcony overlooking the Monterchise countryside.</p>"
_:na1f21f1e030f4c34826db8a014c682b5xb0	http://schema.org/name	"Civic Museums Madonna del Parto"
_:n8d010a91fb834da2a975df6974358ebfxb0	http://schema.org/description	"<p>The Bode-Museum is an important museum in Berlin. <span style=&quot;font-size: 1rem;&quot;>It is located inside the so-called &quot;Museum Island&quot;,</span> <span style=&quot;font-size: 1rem;&quot;>declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Founded in 1904, the</span> name of the museum derives from its first director, Wilhelm von Bode, to whom we owe the strong impulse to the foundation of the museum: until 1956, in fact, the museum was entitled Kaiser Friedrich Museum, name of the German Emperor at the time of its foundation. The museum houses a vast and important collection divided into two macro-sectors: that of Byzantine Sculptures and Art (Skulpturensammlung und Museum für Byzantinische Kunst) and the numismatic collection (Münzkabinett). The rich Byzantine collection includes works from the different regions of the Mediterranean basin of the ancient Byzantine Empire: from Constantinople to Greece, from the Balkans to North African countries. A series of Russian icons complete the collection. Of the sculpture collection, the rooms of the German Late Gothic and those of the Italian Renaissance are particularly interesting, with the terracottas by Luca della Robbia and other masterpieces by Donatello and Desiderio da Settignano. The Numismatic Cabinet boasts the largest collection of &quot;numismatic&quot; coins in the world, from the 7th century BC to the present day, counting over 500,000 pieces, while the coins prior to the 7th century are instead kept in the Altes Museum. </p><p><br></p><section class=&quot;detailpage-map&quot; style=&quot;box-sizing: inherit; margin-bottom: 2.1875rem; width: 620.656px; overflow: visible; position: relative; margin-top: 2.1875rem;&quot;><h3 class=&quot;aural&quot; style=&quot;box-sizing: inherit; font-weight: bold; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); border: 0px none; font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.25em; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; position: absolute; top: -1000px; width: 1px; overflow-wrap: break-word; margin-bottom: 0px !important; margin-right: 0px !important; margin-left: 0px !important; padding: 0px !important;&quot;> Karte</h3></section><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><span style=&quot;font-size: 1rem;&quot;> </span></p>"
_:n8d010a91fb834da2a975df6974358ebfxb0	http://schema.org/name	"Bode-Museum"
_:na8d151f90e7e468f81b576afbc01a686xb0	http://schema.org/description	"<p>The museum preserves materials related to findings in the territory of the Municipality of Cingoli and to the excavations carried out in the 1980s in the area where there is now an artificial reservoir. The dating of the artifacts ranges from the Lower Paleolithic period, with a rich display of lithic artifacts, to the Roman Age, to which twelve lapidary inscriptions, fragments of statues, glass balsamaria, and coins refer. For the Bronze Age, in addition to a casting mold, several dagger blades and pottery, a wide range of tools made of deer horn are also highlighted.</p><p>The museum preserves materials related to findings in the territory of the Municipality of Cingoli and to the excavations carried out in the 1980s in the area where there is now an artificial reservoir. The dating of the artifacts ranges from the Lower Paleolithic period, with a rich display of lithic artifacts, to the Roman Age, to which twelve lapidary inscriptions, fragments of statues, glass balsamaria, and coins refer. For the Bronze Age, in addition to a casting mold, several dagger blades and pottery, a wide range of tools made of deer horn are also highlighted.</p>"
